# What types of plant introductions were influenced by urban land use?

## Data
- invaded_niche_pathways.csv: 632 plots (Barcelona province); 191 with >=1 alien species;
  per-pathway counts (Gardening 169, Unintentional 289, AgriForest 23 records).
- invasion_success_pathways.csv: 81 species (77 after dropping 4 duplicated rows);
  pathway: Gardening 42, Unintentional 30, AgriForest 9 (raw) / 39,29,9 (unique).
- temporal_trends_contingency_table.csv: Catalonia-wide period x pathway counts.
- phylogenetic_tree.txt: 81 tips, 1:1 match with phylo.tip.label (robustness only).

## Methods
Firth-penalized binomial GLMs (regularization handles the perfect separation found
in habitat strata, e.g. Gardening present in ALL plots of broad-leaved/coniferous
forests; AgriForest absent from 4 whole habitat strata). Primary exposure
urban_2009_500m; sensitivity: urban_1956_500m, urban_2009_50m, urban_2009_1000m;
secondary: progressive landscape-change fractions. Covariates: elevation, habitat
(10 levels), annual temperature/rainfall, log distance to road/stream.
Diagnostics: Wald CIs, LRT p-values, residual Moran's I (k=8 NN, 199 permutations).

## Key results
1. Composition (191 occupied plots), urban_2009_500m coefficient [Wald 95% CI] (LRT p):
   - Unintentional: +0.74 [-0.27, +1.74] (p=0.14)  -> positive
   - Gardening:     -0.80 [-1.82, +0.22] (p=0.11)  -> negative
   - AgriForest:    +0.09 [-2.54, +2.72] (p=1.00)  -> null
   Sensitivity: Unintentional positive and consistent at 500 m (+0.74) and 1000 m
   (+0.73) buffers for 2009 cover, null at 50 m and for 1956 cover. Progressive
   change: no significant term (all p>0.15).
2. Urban-habitat enrichment of pathway records: Unintentional 36.0% of records in
   Urban habitat vs 28.3% baseline (ratio 1.27; mean urban_2009_500m of records 0.33
   vs 0.30); Gardening 26.6% (ratio 0.94); AgriForest 8.7% (ratio 0.31).
   Gardening vs Unintentional difference: chi2 = 3.84, p = 0.05.
3. Temporal (Catalonia, descriptive): Gardening share of introductions rises from
   16.8% (Before 1500) to 75.2% (1985-2019); Unintentional peaks 1901-1984 (37.9%).
4. Species level: Unintentional species invade most plots (mean 9.5 vs 4.0 Gardening,
   2.3 AgriForest), widest habitat range (2.72) and niche breadth (0.75), and are
   almost exclusively herbs (0% trees/shrubs); Gardening species are 44% trees/shrubs.
5. Spatial autocorrelation: composition-model residuals show no significant
   autocorrelation (Moran's I 0.01/0.02/-0.02, p>0.45); overall presence residuals
   ARE autocorrelated (I=0.358, p=0.005) - only partially treatable (no plot IDs,
   no full spatial model possible).
6. Phylogenetic robustness: Unintentional species are phylogenetically clustered
   (within-pathway patristic distance 183 vs null 208, p=0.001) - a coherent
   urban/ruderal clade (Amaranthus, Erigeron, Symphyotrichum, Xanthium...);
   Gardening species are phylogenetically dispersed (216 vs 208, p=0.995).

## Answer
Urban land use has mainly influenced (i) UNINTENTIONAL introductions at the
establishment/landscape scale - accidental arrivals are over-represented in and
around urbanized plots - and (ii) GARDENING introductions at the introduction-effort
scale over time, not at the local establishment scale: the post-1985 explosion of
ornamental introductions coincides with urban growth, but established gardening
species are not concentrated near urban cover. Agriculture/forestry introductions
show no urban association and are urban-avoiding.
